package gates

// architecture is the code statute on data model, state ownership, and
// boundaries. One body is one bullet or one short paragraph of the source
// text, with markdown stripped. Each body is at most maxBody code points.
var architecture = []provision{
	{"§1", "Data model", "Everything else inherits its shape."},
	{"§1.1", "Domain truth", "Goal: the smallest model that represents the domain truthfully. A wrong entity or relationship leaks into every query, API, and screen."},
	{"§1.2", "Entities are real domain concepts", "Every entity MUST correspond to a real domain concept that can be named and defined in one sentence. No entities that exist only to serve one screen or one endpoint."},
	{"§1.3", "Each fact stored once", "Every fact MUST be stored in exactly one place. Derived values (totals, counts, status computed from other fields) are computed, not stored, unless there is a measured performance need, and then the stored copy is explicitly marked as derived with one documented refresh path."},
	{"§1.4", "Stable, meaningless identifiers", "Every entity MUST have a stable, immutable identifier that carries no business meaning. Never use mutable or meaningful values (email, name, composite of user-editable fields) as primary keys."},
	{"§1.5", "Explicit, enforced relationships", "Relationships MUST be explicit and enforced: foreign keys, correct cardinality, and a defined behaviour on delete. No relationships held only in application code or in ID lists inside a text or JSON field."},
	{"§1.6", "Invariants at the lowest layer", "Invariants MUST be enforced at the lowest layer that can enforce them: NOT NULL, unique constraints, check constraints, and enums or lookup tables in the schema; anything the schema cannot express in the domain layer. Never rely on the UI alone."},
	{"§1.7", "Illegal states unrepresentable", "Illegal states MUST be unrepresentable where practical. Prefer one status field with defined transitions over several booleans that can contradict each other (for example is_active, is_deleted, is_archived)."},
	{"§1.8", "Versioned, safe migrations", "Schema changes MUST ship as versioned, reviewable migrations that are safe to run against existing data. State the backfill and rollback plan for any change to existing columns."},
	{"§1.9", "Non-nullable by default", "Columns SHOULD be non-nullable by default. Each nullable column needs a stated meaning for null."},
	{"§1.10", "Precise types", "Use precise types: timestamps with time zone stored in UTC, exact decimals or integer minor units for money, never floats for money or strings for dates."},
	{"§1.11", "No catch-all structures", "Avoid generic catch-all structures (untyped JSON blobs, entity-attribute-value tables, metadata columns) for data that is queried, validated, or relied on by logic."},
	{"§1.12", "One domain name per concept", "Name things in the domain's language, consistently, one name per concept across schema, code, and API."},
	{"§1.13", "No speculative fields", "Do not add fields or tables for speculative future needs. Add them when the need exists."},
	{"§1.14", "Index real access paths", "Index for the access paths that actually exist; every foreign key used in joins or lookups gets an index."},
	{"§1.15", "Red flag: duplicated value", "The same value appears in two tables or two columns."},
	{"§1.16", "Red flag: field depends on another", "A field whose meaning depends on another field's value."},
	{"§1.17", "Red flag: table named for a screen", "A table named after a screen, report, or endpoint."},
	{"§1.18", "Red flag: booleans for one lifecycle", "Several booleans describing one lifecycle."},
	{"§1.19", "Red flag: payload unlike the model", "An API payload shaped differently from the model with no stated reason."},

	{"§2", "State ownership", "Each fact has exactly one owner and one write path."},
	{"§2.1", "One answer per fact", `Goal: for every fact, anyone can answer "who owns it and how does it change?" with one answer. This applies to database rows, in-memory state, client or UI state, caches, and data held in external services.`},
	{"§2.2", "One owning module", "Every piece of state MUST have exactly one owning module or service. Only the owner writes it."},
	{"§2.3", "One write path per fact", "There MUST be exactly one write path per fact: a single function, command, or endpoint through which all changes pass, where validation and invariants are applied. No direct writes that bypass it (ad hoc SQL updates from other modules, direct mutation of another module's objects or store)."},
	{"§2.4", "Read through the owner", "Other modules MUST read through the owner's interface or receive the data from it. They do not reach into the owner's tables or internals."},
	{"§2.5", "Copies are derived", "Any copy of state (cache, denormalized column, client-side copy, search index, replica in another service) MUST be explicitly treated as derived: it names its source, is never written independently, and has a defined invalidation or refresh mechanism."},
	{"§2.6", "Client state is not a second source", "Client or UI state MUST NOT duplicate server state as a second source of truth. Server data on the client is a cache of the server's answer; local-only state (form drafts, selection, open panels) stays local."},
	{"§2.7", "Consistent changes are atomic", "Changes that must be consistent MUST happen in one transaction or have an explicit, designed mechanism for eventual consistency (outbox, idempotent events, reconciliation). Never two independent writes that are hoped to both succeed."},
	{"§2.8", "Retried writes are idempotent", "Writes that may be retried MUST be idempotent."},
	{"§2.9", "Derive over synchronize", "Prefer deriving over synchronizing. If a value can be computed from owned state, compute it."},
	{"§2.10", "Keep state local", "Keep state as local as it can be: local variable before component state, component state before shared store, shared store before global."},
	{"§2.11", "No mutable globals", "Avoid mutable global and module-level singletons. Pass state and dependencies explicitly."},
	{"§2.12", "Two writers means wrong ownership", "When two modules both seem to need to write the same fact, the ownership is wrong: move the fact, or route one module's changes as requests to the owner."},
	{"§2.13", "Red flag: two paths update a field", "Two code paths that update the same field."},
	{"§2.14", "Red flag: code that keeps values in sync", "Code that exists to keep two values in sync."},
	{"§2.15", "Red flag: stale value bugs", `A bug described as stale, out of sync, or "sometimes shows the old value".`},
	{"§2.16", "Red flag: cache without invalidation", "A cache with no documented invalidation."},
	{"§2.17", "Red flag: edited copy of server data", "A component that fetches server data, copies it into local state, and edits the copy."},

	{"§3", "Boundaries", "The core domain is independent of UI, database, and frameworks."},
	{"§3.1", "Small change, local change", "Goal: a small change in requirements produces a small, local change in code."},
	{"§3.2", "Dependencies point inward", "Dependencies MUST point inward: UI, API handlers, persistence, and external integrations depend on the core domain. The core domain MUST NOT import UI code, ORM or database clients, HTTP or web framework types, or vendor SDKs."},
	{"§3.3", "Core defines its interfaces", "The core MUST reach the outside world only through interfaces it defines (for example a repository or gateway interface), implemented by the outer layers and passed in."},
	{"§3.4", "Acyclic module graph", "The module dependency graph MUST be acyclic. If A needs B and B needs A, extract the shared concept or invert one dependency."},
	{"§3.5", "Public interfaces only", "Modules MUST interact only through each other's public interface. No imports of another module's internal files, no reads of another module's tables."},
	{"§3.6", "Translate types at the boundary", "Framework, transport, and storage types (request objects, ORM rows, SDK responses) MUST be translated at the boundary and never passed into or returned from the core."},
	{"§3.7", "Business rules in the core", "Business rules MUST live in the core domain, not in controllers, UI components, SQL triggers, or integration glue."},
	{"§3.8", "Narrow interfaces", "Keep interfaces narrow: few methods, expressed in domain terms, hiding how the work is done."},
	{"§3.9", "Side effects at the edges", "Keep side effects (I/O, network, clock, randomness) at the edges; keep core logic deterministic and testable without mocks of infrastructure."},
	{"§3.10", "One responsibility per module", "Each module has one responsibility that can be stated in a sentence. If it cannot, split it."},
	{"§3.11", "No pass-through layers", "Do not add layers that only pass calls through, or abstractions with a single implementation and no boundary to protect. A boundary earns its place by isolating something that changes independently."},
	{"§3.12", "Organize by domain capability", "Organize code by domain capability rather than by technical type when the codebase allows it."},
	{"§3.13", "Encode dependency rules", "Where tooling exists (import linting, dependency rules, architecture tests), encode the dependency rules so violations fail automatically."},
	{"§3.14", "Red flag: domain imports a framework", "A domain file importing a framework, ORM, or SDK."},
	{"§3.15", "Red flag: one feature, many modules", "A change to one feature that touches many modules."},
	{"§3.16", "Red flag: growing shared module", `A "utils", "common", or "shared" module that everything depends on and that keeps growing.`},
	{"§3.17", "Red flag: domain tests need infra", "Domain tests that need a database or HTTP server to run."},
	{"§3.18", "Red flag: interface mirrors quirks", "An interface that mirrors one implementation's quirks."},

	{"§4", "How to apply", "Before writing code, read the schema, the module layout, and where the affected state is written."},
	{"§4.1", "Read before designing", "Read the existing schema, the module layout, and the places where the affected state is written. Do not design from assumptions."},
	{"§4.2", "State the change", "State in a few lines: which entities change, who owns each new fact, and which module the code belongs in."},
	{"§4.3", "Resolve hard rules first", "Run the checks below. Resolve any hard-rule violation before implementing."},
	{"§4.4", "Review with the same checks", `When reviewing code or a design, run the same checks and report findings in the format under "Reporting".`},
	{"§4.5", "No unrelated refactors", "Do not refactor unrelated code to comply; flag pre-existing violations separately from the change at hand."},

	{"§5", "Severity", "Hard rules are MUST. Defaults are SHOULD."},
	{"§5.1", "Hard rules block", "Hard rules marked MUST: do not write or approve code that violates them."},
	{"§5.2", "Stop on a required violation", "If a request requires a violation, stop, explain the cost in one or two sentences, and propose the compliant alternative. Proceed only if the user explicitly accepts it, and record the exception."},
	{"§5.3", "Defaults need a stated reason", "Defaults marked SHOULD: follow unless there is a concrete reason not to; state the reason when deviating."},
	{"§5.4", "Match existing conventions", "Match the existing codebase's conventions where they already satisfy these rules."},

	{"§6", "Checklist", "A no on a hard rule blocks the change."},
	{"§6.1", "Data model: one-sentence entities", "Can each new or changed entity be defined in one sentence in domain terms?"},
	{"§6.2", "Data model: each fact stored once", "Is every new fact stored exactly once?"},
	{"§6.3", "Data model: enforced in the schema", "Are identifiers, relationships, and constraints enforced in the schema?"},
	{"§6.4", "Data model: contradictory state", "Can any contradictory state be represented? If so, can the model prevent it?"},
	{"§6.5", "Data model: safe migration", "Is there a safe migration with a backfill and rollback plan?"},
	{"§6.6", "State ownership: single owner", "Who is the single owner of each fact touched by this change?"},
	{"§6.7", "State ownership: one write path", "Is there one write path, and does this change use it?"},
	{"§6.8", "State ownership: copies are derived", "Is every copy marked as derived with a refresh or invalidation mechanism?"},
	{"§6.9", "State ownership: transactional writes", "Are multi-step writes transactional or explicitly designed for eventual consistency?"},
	{"§6.10", "Boundaries: the right module", "Which module does this belong in, and why?"},
	{"§6.11", "Boundaries: imports point inward", "Do all new imports point inward, with no cycle introduced?"},
	{"§6.12", "Boundaries: types translated at the edge", "Are external types translated at the edge?"},
	{"§6.13", "Boundaries: business rule in the core", "Is the business rule in the core domain?"},

	{"§7", "Reporting", "Report each finding as the rule, where, why it matters, and the fix."},
	{"§7.1", "Rule", "Rule: the area and the rule violated."},
	{"§7.2", "Where", "Where: file and line, table, or design element."},
	{"§7.3", "Why it matters", `Why it matters: the concrete failure it leads to (for example "two write paths to order.status, so a refund can overwrite a shipment update").`},
	{"§7.4", "Fix", "Fix: the smallest change that restores compliance."},
	{"§7.5", "Finding order", "Order findings hard rules first, then defaults. Keep pre-existing violations in a separate list from those introduced by the change. If there are no findings, say so in one line."},

	{"§8", "Exceptions", "A rule may be broken deliberately, never silently."},
	{"§8.1", "Comment at the site", "Add a short comment at the site stating what rule is broken, why, and what would allow it to be removed."},
	{"§8.2", "Contain the violation", "Contain it: keep the violation behind an interface so the rest of the code does not depend on it."},
	{"§8.3", "Mention it in the summary", "Mention it in the summary of the change."},
	{"§8.4", "Legitimate exceptions", "Typical legitimate exceptions: a denormalized column or cache justified by measured performance, a temporary dual write during a migration, a prototype explicitly marked as throwaway."},
}
